---
Part VI, Chapter 3: The projection pass
Package: packages/loom-lang/src/ast/LoomVirtualCodeBuilder.ts
Language: TypeScript
---

# The projection pass

`LoomVirtualCodeBuilder` produces the `LoomVirtualCode` a `.loom` presents to Volar, in two projections that share one model. `fromProduct` walks a section's `Code` and emits its de re product, inlining transclusions by following `Ref`s across the corpus. `fromProse` blanks a file's code and emits what remains as Markdown. `fromProduct` folds its pieces with a monoid, and `rootVirtualCode` assembles a file's tree — the source as the de dicto root, the product sections and prose beneath it. Pure and total.

## Resolving against the corpus

`fromProduct` resolves a `Ref`'s `{ path, name }` target against the corpus modules. `lookup` finds the module at the path, takes its de re `product`, and finds the `Code` whose `origin` carries the name. A module the run has not produced yet, or one with no section by that name, resolves to nothing.

A `Ref` the product pass left open has no target yet. Its name lies beyond the loom that holds it, so `inlinePart` binds it first through the product pass's `targetOf`, which resolves the name through the corpus's `Catalogue` from that loom.

=>

const lookup = (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>, id: SectionId): Option.Option<Code> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(id.path)),
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((m) => m.product),
    Option.flatMap((product) => Array.findFirst(product.code, (c) => c.origin.name === id.name)),
  )

## The fold monoid

A childless `LoomVirtualCode` is the unit a walk produces and folds. `concat` appends one after another, shifting the later one's mapping offsets past the earlier's code; the first's identity wins. `leaf` makes a unit, and `emptyLeaf` is the seed.

=>

const leaf = (
  id: string,
  languageId: string,
  code: string,
  mappings: ReadonlyArray<Mapping>,
): LoomVirtualCode => ({ id, languageId, code, mappings, embeddedCodes: [] })

const emptyLeaf: LoomVirtualCode = leaf('', '', '', [])

const concat = (a: LoomVirtualCode, b: LoomVirtualCode): LoomVirtualCode => ({
  id: a.id,
  languageId: a.languageId,
  code: a.code + b.code,
  mappings: [
    ...a.mappings,
    ...b.mappings.map((m) => ({
      ...m,
      genStart: m.genStart + a.code.length,
    })),
  ],
  embeddedCodes: a.embeddedCodes,
})

## Inlining the product

The de re walk inlines a section's fragments. `inlineComposed` folds them left to right, carrying both the text emitted so far and each fragment's successor, so it can tell whether an anchor stands alone on its line. A `Ref` that does is re-indented to that line's indent (the next section), and every other fragment — an anchor with code beside it among them — is appended as it stands. The fold also carries a flag. After an anchor that owned its line, the fragment that follows opens with the spaces the author left past the anchor, so the fold trims them.

`inlinePart` handles each fragment. A `Fragment` becomes a `product` leaf, and a composing edge is bound, followed, and inlined, contributing nothing when its target is unresolved, missing, or cyclic. It learns which loom holds the edge from the section being folded, the `owner`, because an open edge resolves its name from there. `absorbTrailingNewline`, with `clipMappings`, sheds a transcluded block's trailing newline so the sink's layout becomes the output's.

A module anchor, which the product pass binds as a `ModuleRef`, composes no code. It writes the path to the module it names, the specifier the language package wrote, and `Specifiers` hands the walk those specifiers. The compiler works them out before it projects, because finding the file a module anchor names, placing it, and asking the package all take effects, and the walk is pure. A module anchor with no specifier writes nothing. The specifier maps onto the anchor's name, although the two differ in length, so an error the language reports on the specifier lands on the anchor that wrote it. `noSpecifiers` gives no module anchor a specifier, for a projection that writes none.

A leaf maps to its origin only when its section lives in the file being projected, the `home` file the walk carries down from its root. A mapping is a span in that one file's source, while a `Fragment`'s origin is a span in the file its own section came from. So a block that a cross-file anchor pulls in still lands in the code, and the product type-checks whole, but the block maps nowhere in the home file. Its own file projects it again, with mappings of its own, and an edit to that block belongs there. `withPart` adds one part to the code composed so far, an `Inlined`: the code, and whether the next fragment loses its leading blank line.

=>

const clipMappings = (mappings: ReadonlyArray<Mapping>, genLen: number): ReadonlyArray<Mapping> =>
  Array.getSomes(
    mappings.map((m) =>
      m.genStart >= genLen
        ? Option.none()
        : Option.some(
            m.genStart + m.genLength <= genLen ? m : { ...m, genLength: genLen - m.genStart },
          ),
    ),
  )

const absorbTrailingNewline = (vc: LoomVirtualCode): LoomVirtualCode => {
  const code = vc.code.replace(/\n+$/, '')
  return code.length === vc.code.length
    ? vc
    : { ...vc, code, mappings: clipMappings(vc.mappings, code.length) }
}

export type Specifiers = (owner: Location, ref: ModuleRef) => Option.Option<string>

export const noSpecifiers: Specifiers = () => Option.none()

const mappedIn = (text: string, source: Position, owner: Location, home: Location) =>
  leaf(
    '',
    '',
    text,
    owner === home ? [{ genStart: 0, genLength: text.length, source, kind: 'product' }] : [],
  )

const inlinePart = byType<
  Part,
  [
    within: {
      readonly modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>
      readonly catalogue: Catalogue
      readonly seen: ReadonlySet<string>
      readonly home: Location
      readonly owner: Location
      readonly specifiers: Specifiers
    },
  ]
>()<LoomVirtualCode>({
  Fragment: (fragment, { home, owner }) =>
    mappedIn(fragment.text, fragment.origin, owner, home),
  ModuleRef: (ref, { home, owner, specifiers }) =>
    Option.match(specifiers(owner, ref), {
      onNone: () => emptyLeaf,
      onSome: (specifier) => mappedIn(specifier, ref.anchor, owner, home),
    }),
  NameRef: (ref, { modules, catalogue, seen, home, owner, specifiers }) =>
    pipe(
      targetOf(catalogue, owner, ref),
      Option.filter((t) => !seen.has(keyOf(t))),
      Option.flatMap((t) => Option.map(lookup(modules, t), (node) => [t, node] as const)),
      Option.match({
        onNone: () => emptyLeaf,
        onSome: ([t, node]) =>
          absorbTrailingNewline(
            inlineComposed(
              modules,
              catalogue,
              new Set([...seen, keyOf(t)]),
              home,
              specifiers,
            )(node),
          ),
      }),
    ),
})

type Inlined = { readonly vc: LoomVirtualCode; readonly trim: boolean }

const withPart = byType<
  Part,
  [
    within: {
      readonly acc: Inlined
      readonly build: (part: Part) => LoomVirtualCode
      readonly node: Code
      readonly i: number
    },
  ]
>()<Inlined>({
  Fragment: (fragment, { acc, build }) => ({
    vc: concat(acc.vc, build(acc.trim ? trimLeadingBlank(fragment) : fragment)),
    trim: false,
  }),
  ModuleRef: (ref, { acc, build }) =>
    ({ vc: concat(acc.vc, build(ref)), trim: false }),
  NameRef: (ref, { acc, build, node, i }) => {
    const owns = aloneBefore(acc.vc.code) && aloneAfter(node.fragments[i + 1])
    return {
      vc: concat(
        acc.vc,
        owns ? indentBlock(build(ref), lineIndentOf(acc.vc.code)) : build(ref),
      ),
      trim: owns,
    }
  },
})

const inlineComposed =
  (
    modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
    catalogue: Catalogue,
    seen: ReadonlySet<string>,
    home: Location,
    specifiers: Specifiers,
  ) =>
  (node: Code): LoomVirtualCode => {
    const build = (part: Part): LoomVirtualCode =>
      inlinePart(part, { modules, catalogue, seen, home, owner: node.origin.path, specifiers })
    const seed: Inlined = { vc: emptyLeaf, trim: false }
    return pipe(
      node.fragments.reduce((acc, part, i) => withPart(part, { acc, build, node, i }), seed),
      (acc) => acc.vc,
    )
  }

## What a composition leaves to be settled

A tangle writes no file whose composition reaches a section that holds no code yet. `unsettledIn` walks a root's composition the way `inlineComposed` does, binding each reference with `targetOf` and following it into the section it reaches. It reports an `Unsettled` reference at each edge that stops short of code: the loom that holds the anchor, the anchor's span, the name it writes, the section it reaches when a section of that name is declared, and the names the anchor says the section provides. `unsettledRef` judges one reference on its own, and the weave asks it too, to mark the woven page. `unsettledAt` builds the report from the loom that holds the reference. A reference that names a trope still to be settled promises the names the trope's box lists, beside any its anchor lists, which the corpus's `promisedThrough` gives. So code that uses a trope before anyone writes it sees the trope's names as promised.

An edge stops short in two ways. It reaches a section whose `Code` holds no fragments, a section declared but not yet written. Or it binds to nothing while its anchor promises the section, with the `{To be settled}` label or by stating what the section provides, so the section is not declared at all. `isMeant` asks whether an edge promises its section. An edge that binds to nothing without a promise is a fault the diagnostics already raise, so the walk passes it by. A section the walk has already entered is not entered again, which ends a cycle.

A module anchor stops short when the module it names is to be settled: a chapter that emits no file yet, or a name nobody has declared that its anchor promises. `unsettledModuleRef` asks the product pass's `moduleFileOf`. It reports the module anchor with `moduleAnchor` set, and with `declared` saying whether its name finds a chapter or a section. A sinkless section's landing decides only which file it names, never whether a module is to be settled, so the walk hands in an empty landing. The walk never follows a module anchor, since the code it names lands in another file. `unsettledInPart` gives what one part of a root leaves unsettled.

=>

export type Unsettled = {
  readonly path: Location
  readonly anchor: Position
  readonly name: string
  readonly section: Option.Option<SectionId>
  readonly provides: ReadonlyArray<Provided>
  readonly moduleAnchor: boolean
  readonly declared: boolean
}

const unsettledAt = (owner: Location, ref: Ref, section: Option.Option<SectionId>): Unsettled => ({
  path: owner,
  anchor: ref.anchor,
  name: ref.name,
  section,
  provides: ref.provides,
  moduleAnchor: false,
  declared: Option.isSome(section),
})

const unsettledModuleRef = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  host: Code,
  ref: ModuleRef,
): Option.Option<Unsettled> =>
  Option.flatMap(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(host.origin.path)), (module) =>
    ModuleFile.$match(moduleFileOf(catalogue, modules, {}, module, host, ref), {
      Unsettled: ({ declared }) =>
        Option.some<Unsettled>({
          path: host.origin.path,
          anchor: ref.anchor,
          name: ref.name,
          section: Option.none(),
          provides: ref.provides,
          moduleAnchor: true,
          declared,
        }),
      Named: () => Option.none(),
      Faulted: () => Option.none(),
    }),
  )

export const unsettledRef = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  owner: Location,
  ref: Ref,
): Option.Option<Unsettled> =>
  Option.match(targetOf(catalogue, owner, ref), {
    onNone: () => {
      const promised = promisedThrough(catalogue, owner, ref.name)
      return isMeant(ref) || Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(promised)
        ? Option.some(
            unsettledAt(owner, { ...ref, provides: [...ref.provides, ...promised] }, Option.none()),
          )
        : Option.none()
    },
    onSome: (target) =>
      pipe(
        lookup(modules, target),
        Option.filter((code) => !Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(code.fragments)),
        Option.map(() => unsettledAt(owner, ref, Option.some(target))),
      ),
  })

const unsettledInPart = byType<
  Part,
  [
    within: {
      readonly modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>
      readonly catalogue: Catalogue
      readonly seen: ReadonlySet<string>
      readonly node: Code
    },
  ]
>()<ReadonlyArray<Unsettled>>({
  Fragment: (): ReadonlyArray<Unsettled> => [],
  ModuleRef: (ref, { modules, catalogue, node }) =>
    Option.toArray(unsettledModuleRef(modules, catalogue, node, ref)),
  NameRef: (ref, { modules, catalogue, seen, node }) =>
    Option.match(unsettledRef(modules, catalogue, node.origin.path, ref), {
      onSome: (unsettled): ReadonlyArray<Unsettled> => [unsettled],
      onNone: () =>
        pipe(
          targetOf(catalogue, node.origin.path, ref),
          Option.filter((reached) => !seen.has(keyOf(reached))),
          Option.flatMap((reached) =>
            Option.map(lookup(modules, reached), (code) =>
              unsettledFrom(modules, catalogue, new Set([...seen, keyOf(reached)]))(code),
            ),
          ),
          Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<Unsettled> => []),
        ),
    }),
})

const unsettledFrom =
  (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>, catalogue: Catalogue, seen: ReadonlySet<string>) =>
  (node: Code): ReadonlyArray<Unsettled> =>
    node.fragments.flatMap((part) => unsettledInPart(part, { modules, catalogue, seen, node }))

export const unsettledIn = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  root: Code,
  catalogue: Catalogue = catalogueOf(modules),
): ReadonlyArray<Unsettled> =>
  unsettledFrom(modules, catalogue, new Set([keyOf(root.origin)]))(root)

## The module anchors a composition holds

A root's composition may hold module anchors in its own code and in the code of every section it composes, and each needs a specifier before the root is projected. `moduleRefsIn` walks the composition as `unsettledIn` does and gathers a `HeldModuleRef` for each one: the module anchor as its `ref`, the section that holds the module anchor as its `host`, and the loom that holds the host as its `owner`. The compiler works out a specifier for each one, written from the file the root lands in. `moduleRefsInPart` gives the module anchors that one part holds or reaches.

=>

export interface HeldModuleRef {
  readonly owner: Location
  readonly host: Code
  readonly ref: ModuleRef
}

const moduleRefsInPart = byType<
  Part,
  [
    within: {
      readonly modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>
      readonly catalogue: Catalogue
      readonly seen: ReadonlySet<string>
      readonly node: Code
    },
  ]
>()<ReadonlyArray<HeldModuleRef>>({
  Fragment: (): ReadonlyArray<HeldModuleRef> => [],
  ModuleRef: (ref, { node }): ReadonlyArray<HeldModuleRef> => [
    { owner: node.origin.path, host: node, ref },
  ],
  NameRef: (ref, { modules, catalogue, seen, node }) =>
    pipe(
      targetOf(catalogue, node.origin.path, ref),
      Option.filter((reached) => !seen.has(keyOf(reached))),
      Option.flatMap((reached) =>
        Option.map(lookup(modules, reached), (code) =>
          moduleRefsFrom(modules, catalogue, new Set([...seen, keyOf(reached)]))(code),
        ),
      ),
      Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<HeldModuleRef> => []),
    ),
})

const moduleRefsFrom =
  (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>, catalogue: Catalogue, seen: ReadonlySet<string>) =>
  (node: Code): ReadonlyArray<HeldModuleRef> =>
    node.fragments.flatMap((part) => moduleRefsInPart(part, { modules, catalogue, seen, node }))

export const moduleRefsIn = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  root: Code,
  catalogue: Catalogue = catalogueOf(modules),
): ReadonlyArray<HeldModuleRef> =>
  moduleRefsFrom(modules, catalogue, new Set([keyOf(root.origin)]))(root)

## The names a composition cannot find

A composition leaves an anchor's place empty when the catalogue it is composed against holds no section of the anchor's name. The composed text shows no trace of the gap, so a reader that needs the whole text asks for the missing names first. `unfoundIn` walks a root's composition as `unsettledIn` does. It gives each name that `targetOf` finds no section for, in the root's own code and in the code of every section the root composes. The registry of declared kinds asks it before it reads a trope's manifest, as the chapter "The kinds tropes declare" describes. `unfoundInPart` gives the names that one part cannot find.

=>

const unfoundInPart = byType<
  Part,
  [
    within: {
      readonly modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>
      readonly catalogue: Catalogue
      readonly seen: ReadonlySet<string>
      readonly node: Code
    },
  ]
>()<ReadonlyArray<string>>({
  Fragment: (): ReadonlyArray<string> => [],
  ModuleRef: (): ReadonlyArray<string> => [],
  NameRef: (ref, { modules, catalogue, seen, node }) =>
    Option.match(targetOf(catalogue, node.origin.path, ref), {
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<string> => [ref.name],
      onSome: (reached) =>
        pipe(
          Option.some(reached),
          Option.filter((found) => !seen.has(keyOf(found))),
          Option.flatMap((found) => lookup(modules, found)),
          Option.map(unfoundFrom(modules, catalogue, new Set([...seen, keyOf(reached)]))),
          Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<string> => []),
        ),
    }),
})

const unfoundFrom =
  (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>, catalogue: Catalogue, seen: ReadonlySet<string>) =>
  (node: Code): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
    node.fragments.flatMap((part) => unfoundInPart(part, { modules, catalogue, seen, node }))

export const unfoundIn = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  root: Code,
  catalogue: Catalogue = catalogueOf(modules),
): ReadonlyArray<string> => unfoundFrom(modules, catalogue, new Set([keyOf(root.origin)]))(root)

## What an anchor says its section provides

An anchor may state the names its section provides, as `::[Greeting function]{provides=greet}` does. A `Contract` is what one such anchor expects of the section it composes: the `section` as the anchor names it, the loom that holds the section as `target`, the `span` of the section's own code in that loom, the `language` that code is written in, and each `Provided` name with the place the anchor writes it. A trope box makes a contract too, with its entry point, and the contract then names the `trope`, so a missing name is laid at the trope's door rather than an anchor's. A language service reports the names a document declares, and the editor compares them with a contract, as the chapter "The Loom language" describes.

`contractsIn` gathers the contracts of one loom. It walks every composing edge the loom's product holds, and `contractOf` builds the contract of one from the `provides` names the reference records. It adds the contract of each trope box the loom holds, which `tropeContractOf` builds from the corpus's `tropeContractsIn`: the code of the trope's entry point against the names the box promises. `writtenSpan` runs from the section's first fragment of code to its last. Only a section that holds code of its own has names to compare, so a section to be settled, one that is missing, or one that only composes others holds no contract. The span covers the section's own code alone, so a name that a section it composes declares is not among the names the section provides.

A module anchor states the names its module provides the same way, as `import { greet } from ::[Greetings]{provides=greet}` does, and its contract covers the file it names. `moduleContractOf` builds it once the product pass's `moduleFileOf` finds the file. A module is the file's root and every section the root composes, so `composedSpan` runs from the first fragment of code among them to the last, over the sections that live in the root's own loom, which `composedOf` gathers. A section of the file that another loom holds lies outside that span, so a name only such a section declares reads as missing. A module anchor whose module is to be settled, or one a fault stops, holds no contract. `contractsIn` reads the corpus's `Landing` once for every module anchor in the loom. `contractOfPart` reads the contract of one part of a root.

=>

export type Contract = {
  readonly section: string
  readonly target: Location
  readonly span: Position
  readonly language: string
  readonly provides: ReadonlyArray<Provided>
  readonly trope: Option.Option<string>
}

const writtenSpan = (code: Code): Option.Option<Position> =>
  pipe(
    code.fragments.map((part) =>
      part.type === 'Fragment' ? Option.some(part.origin) : Option.none(),
    ),
    Array.getSomes,
    Array.match({
      onEmpty: () => Option.none(),
      onNonEmpty: (origins) =>
        Option.some({
          start: Array.headNonEmpty(origins).start,
          end: Array.lastNonEmpty(origins).end,
        }),
    }),
  )

const contractOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  path: Location,
  ref: Ref,
): Option.Option<Contract> =>
  Option.gen(function* () {
    const provides = yield* Option.liftPredicate(ref.provides, Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty)
    const target = yield* targetOf(catalogue, path, ref)
    const code = yield* lookup(modules, target)
    const span = yield* writtenSpan(code)
    return {
      section: ref.name,
      target: target.path,
      span,
      language: code.languageId,
      provides,
      trope: Option.none(),
    }
  })

const composedOf =
  (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>, catalogue: Catalogue, seen: ReadonlySet<string>) =>
  (node: Code): ReadonlyArray<Code> => [
    node,
    ...node.fragments.flatMap((part) =>
      part.type === 'NameRef'
        ? pipe(
            targetOf(catalogue, node.origin.path, part),
            Option.filter((reached) => !seen.has(keyOf(reached))),
            Option.flatMap((reached) =>
              Option.map(lookup(modules, reached), (code) =>
                composedOf(modules, catalogue, new Set([...seen, keyOf(reached)]))(code),
              ),
            ),
            Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<Code> => []),
          )
        : [],
    ),
  ]

const composedSpan = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  root: Code,
): Option.Option<Position> =>
  pipe(
    composedOf(
      modules,
      catalogue,
      new Set([keyOf(root.origin)]),
    )(root)
      .filter((code) => code.origin.path === root.origin.path)
      .map(writtenSpan),
    Array.getSomes,
    Array.match({
      onEmpty: () => Option.none(),
      onNonEmpty: (spans) =>
        Option.some(
          Array.tailNonEmpty(spans).reduce(
            (whole, span) => ({
              start: span.start.offset < whole.start.offset ? span.start : whole.start,
              end: span.end.offset > whole.end.offset ? span.end : whole.end,
            }),
            Array.headNonEmpty(spans),
          ),
        ),
    }),
  )

const moduleContractOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  landing: Landing,
  module: LoomModule,
  host: Code,
  ref: ModuleRef,
): Option.Option<Contract> =>
  Option.gen(function* () {
    const provides = yield* Option.liftPredicate(ref.provides, Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty)
    const landed = yield* ModuleFile.$match(
      moduleFileOf(catalogue, modules, landing, module, host, ref),
      {
        Named: ({ landed }) => Option.some(landed),
        Unsettled: () => Option.none(),
        Faulted: () => Option.none(),
      },
    )
    const span = yield* composedSpan(modules, catalogue, landed.file.code)
    return {
      section: ref.name,
      target: landed.loom,
      span,
      language: landed.file.code.languageId,
      provides,
      trope: Option.none(),
    }
  })

const tropeContractOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  contract: TropeContract,
): Option.Option<Contract> =>
  Option.gen(function* () {
    const code = yield* lookup(modules, {
      path: contract.entry.module,
      name: sectionNameOf(contract.entry.section),
    })
    const span = yield* writtenSpan(code)
    return {
      section: contract.entry.section.heading.title?.source ?? contract.trope,
      target: contract.entry.module,
      span,
      language: code.languageId,
      provides: contract.provides,
      trope: Option.some(contract.trope),
    }
  })

const contractOfPart = byType<
  Part,
  [
    within: {
      readonly modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>
      readonly catalogue: Catalogue
      readonly path: Location
      readonly landing: Landing
      readonly module: LoomModule
      readonly host: Code
    },
  ]
>()<Option.Option<Contract>>({
  NameRef: (ref, { modules, catalogue, path }) =>
    contractOf(modules, catalogue, path, ref),
  ModuleRef: (ref, { modules, catalogue, landing, module, host }) =>
    moduleContractOf(modules, catalogue, landing, module, host, ref),
  Fragment: () => Option.none<Contract>(),
})

export const contractsIn = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  path: Location,
  catalogue: Catalogue = catalogueOf(modules),
): ReadonlyArray<Contract> =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Contract> => [],
    onSome: (module) => {
      const landing = landingOf(modules)
      return [
        ...Array.getSomes(
          module.product.code.flatMap((host) =>
            host.fragments.map((part) =>
              contractOfPart(part, { modules, catalogue, path, landing, module, host }),
            ),
          ),
        ),
        ...Array.getSomes(
          tropeContractsIn(catalogue, path).map((contract) => tropeContractOf(modules, contract)),
        ),
      ]
    },
  })

## Re-indenting a transcluded block

An anchor that stands alone on its line carries that line's indentation into what it pulls in. Write `::[body]` four spaces deep and every line of the inlined block lands four spaces deep, not just the first. For Python or Scala 3, where indentation marks block structure, this is what lets a transcluded block land at the depth its anchor sits at rather than break the file.

An anchor owns its line when only spaces and tabs sit between the line break and the anchor, and only whitespace between the anchor and the next break. `aloneBefore` and `aloneAfter` test the two sides. The trailing whitespace `aloneAfter` allows is the spacebar an author taps out of habit after the anchor: it never disqualifies the anchor, and `trimLeadingBlank` strikes it from the next fragment so the block's last line carries no stray spaces. An anchor with real code beside it — `result = ::[body]`, or `::[body] more()` — stays inline, its block pulled in as written with no re-indentation.

When the anchor owns its line, the whitespace opening that line is the indent. `lineIndentOf` reads it off the text folded so far, where the current line is whitespace to the anchor, and it is taken verbatim — the spaces and tabs the author wrote, exactly.

`indentBlock` prepends that indent to every line of the block but the first, which already sits after the indent its own fragment wrote. A blank line stays blank — it takes no indent — so the output grows no trailing whitespace.

The inserted indent is synthetic. It has no origin in any `.loom`, so no mapping may claim it, or the editor would map a click on the indent into the source. `reindentMapping` cuts each mapping at the block's line starts, so the indent always falls in the gap between two pieces and never inside one. A piece then shifts by the indentation added on the lines above it, and re-derives its source per line through `advance`, walking the block text to the cut. Every mapping tracks its source one-to-one — each is a `Fragment`'s span — so the same derivation serves them all.

`lineStartsOf` gives each line's offset in the original block, and `newStartsOf` the offset of its content once the indents are in. `lineIndexOf` finds which line a cut falls on.

=>

const aloneBefore = (code: string): boolean =>
  /^[\t ]*$/.test(code.slice(code.lastIndexOf('\n') + 1))

const aloneAfter = (next: Part | undefined): boolean =>
  next === undefined || (next.type === 'Fragment' && /^[\t ]*(?:\n|$)/.test(next.text))

const lineIndentOf = (code: string): string => code.slice(code.lastIndexOf('\n') + 1)

const advance = (base: Position['start'], text: string): Position['start'] => {
  const newlines = text.split('\n').length - 1
  const lastNewline = text.lastIndexOf('\n')
  return {
    line: base.line + newlines,
    column:
      base.column === undefined
        ? undefined
        : newlines === 0
          ? base.column + text.length
          : text.length - lastNewline,
    offset: base.offset + text.length,
  }
}

const trimLeadingBlank = (fragment: Fragment): Fragment => {
  const blank = /^[\t ]+/.exec(fragment.text)
  return blank === null
    ? fragment
    : {
        ...fragment,
        text: fragment.text.slice(blank[0].length),
        origin: {
          ...fragment.origin,
          start: advance(fragment.origin.start, blank[0]),
        },
      }
}

const lineStartsOf = (lines: ReadonlyArray<string>): ReadonlyArray<number> =>
  Array.mapAccum(lines, 0, (start, line) => [start + line.length + 1, start])[1]

const newStartsOf = (
  lines: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  oldStarts: ReadonlyArray<number>,
  width: number,
): ReadonlyArray<number> =>
  Array.mapAccum(Array.zip(lines, oldStarts), 0, (indented, [line, start]) => {
    const next = start !== 0 && line !== '' ? indented + 1 : indented
    return [next, start + width * next]
  })[1]

const lineIndexOf = (starts: ReadonlyArray<number>, offset: number): number =>
  pipe(
    Array.findLastIndex(starts, (s) => s <= offset),
    Option.getOrElse(() => 0),
  )

const reindentMapping = (
  m: Mapping,
  block: string,
  oldStarts: ReadonlyArray<number>,
  newStarts: ReadonlyArray<number>,
): ReadonlyArray<Mapping> => {
  const end = m.genStart + m.genLength
  const cuts = [m.genStart, ...oldStarts.filter((s) => s > m.genStart && s < end), end]
  return Array.zip(cuts, Array.drop(cuts, 1)).map(([a, b]) => {
    const line = lineIndexOf(oldStarts, a)
    return {
      genStart: newStarts[line] + (a - oldStarts[line]),
      genLength: b - a,
      source: {
        start: advance(m.source.start, block.slice(m.genStart, a)),
        end: advance(m.source.start, block.slice(m.genStart, b)),
      },
      kind: m.kind,
    }
  })
}

const indentBlock = (vc: LoomVirtualCode, indent: string): LoomVirtualCode => {
  if (indent.length === 0) return vc
  const lines = vc.code.split('\n')
  if (lines.length <= 1) return vc
  const oldStarts = lineStartsOf(lines)
  const newStarts = newStartsOf(lines, oldStarts, indent.length)
  return {
    ...vc,
    code: lines.map((line, i) => (i === 0 || line === '' ? line : indent + line)).join('\n'),
    mappings: vc.mappings.flatMap((m) => reindentMapping(m, vc.code, oldStarts, newStarts)),
  }
}

## Mapping the symbols

Loom's navigation and colour serve the root mirror alone — the document whose map to the source is the identity. The root carries one whole-file span of the `source` kind, which Volar forwards for verification only, so a cursor on a token reaches no other Loom feature through it. `symbolMappings` adds the spans that route the rest: one span per symbol the document declares, each carrying its kind. `LoomCompiler` reads that kind's profile from `LoomSymbol` and forwards exactly the features it allows, so go-to on a heading, rename of a warp, and colour on an arrow each reach the right token through the root.

`symbolsOf` classifies every token in one pass — the heading titles and anchors, the warps, the specifiers and sinks, the arrows and tildes. `spanOf` makes one identity mapping over a token's span, since the root's code is the source verbatim.

=>

const spanOf = (position: Position, kind: MappingKind): Mapping => ({
  genStart: position.start.offset,
  genLength: position.end.offset - position.start.offset,
  source: position,
  kind,
})

export const symbolMappings = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<Mapping> =>
  symbolsOf(doc).map((symbol) => spanOf(symbol.position, symbol.kind))

## The passes and the tree

`fromProduct` is the de re pass — a section's product in its own language. Its virtual-code id is the section name lowercased, since Volar requires every embedded-code id to be lowercase. A section absent from the corpus yields an empty document. It takes the corpus's `Catalogue` for the open references it binds. A caller that projects many sections over one corpus builds the book once and passes it in, and any other caller lets `fromProduct` build it.

`fromProduct` finds a section by its identity, then hands it to `fromCode`, the same pass started from a `Code` in hand. A tangle sink already holds its own `Code`, so the compiler composes a sink through `fromCode` and never looks the section up again by name. Both take the `Specifiers` the root's module anchors write, and a caller with none to write leaves them out.

`fromProse` is the prose pass — the file as a Markdown document, its `languageId` `prose`, every code chunk blanked to spaces so only the prose shows while its offsets stay put. `proseOf` writes that document in one pass over the text. For each line of code it takes the prose that stands since the line of code before, then the line itself as spaces, which `blank` gives. The prose after the last line of code closes the document. `proseMappings` maps each prose token to its own source span — the document and section preambles, every heading, and the prose between code blocks — so a mapping is precise per token, never a whole line or the whole file. `rootVirtualCode` assembles a file's tree: the source itself as the `loom` root, the prose and product documents its children. The root carries a whole-file identity span of the `source` kind, so Loom's own diagnostics reach the editor on the file the author opened, and the section-symbol spans beside it, so navigation and rename reach each heading and anchor there.

`rootNamesAt` names a file's composition roots — the sections no composing edge in the file folds in. It reads the file's `product.code` from the corpus, the sections the run produced. A module anchor folds nothing in, so it demotes nothing. A composing edge to a section in the same file demotes its target: the target becomes a fragment of the referring section's root, sharing that scope, not a unit of its own. A reference into another file demotes nothing here, because its target belongs to that other file, which names its own roots. The sections left over, named by no same-file reference, are the roots. The editor type-checks them and lets each fold in the fragments it names. The names come back lowercased, to match the de re ids `fromProduct` assigns.

=>

export const fromCode = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  node: Code,
  catalogue: Catalogue = catalogueOf(modules),
  specifiers: Specifiers = noSpecifiers,
): LoomVirtualCode => ({
  ...inlineComposed(
    modules,
    catalogue,
    new Set([keyOf(node.origin)]),
    node.origin.path,
    specifiers,
  )(node),
  id: node.origin.name.toLowerCase(),
  languageId: node.languageId,
})

export const fromProduct = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  root: SectionId,
  catalogue: Catalogue = catalogueOf(modules),
  specifiers: Specifiers = noSpecifiers,
): LoomVirtualCode =>
  pipe(
    lookup(modules, root),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => leaf(root.name.toLowerCase(), '', '', []),
      onSome: (node) => fromCode(modules, node, catalogue, specifiers),
    }),
  )

const isCodeWeft = (weft: { readonly type: string }): boolean =>
  weft.type === 'ArrowWeft' || weft.type === 'CodeWeft'

const proseMappings = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<Mapping> =>
  [
    ...doc.preamble,
    ...doc.sections.flatMap((section) => [
      section.heading,
      ...section.preamble,
      ...section.code.filter((weft) => !isCodeWeft(weft)),
    ]),
  ].map((token) => spanOf(token.position, 'prose'))

const blank = (text: string): string => text.replace(/[^\n]/g, ' ')

const proseOf = (module: LoomModule): string => {
  const spans = module.doc.sections
    .flatMap((section) => section.code)
    .filter(isCodeWeft)
    .map((weft) => [weft.position.start.offset, weft.position.end.offset] as const)
  const ends: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<number> = [0, ...spans.map(([, end]) => end)]
  return [
    ...spans.map(
      ([start, end], at) =>
        module.text.slice(ends[at], start) + blank(module.text.slice(start, end)),
    ),
    module.text.slice(Array.lastNonEmpty(ends)),
  ].join('')
}

export const fromProse = (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>, path: Location): LoomVirtualCode =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)), {
    onNone: () => leaf('prose', 'prose', '', []),
    onSome: (module) => ({
      id: 'prose',
      languageId: 'prose',
      code: proseOf(module),
      mappings: proseMappings(module.doc),
      embeddedCodes: [],
    }),
  })

export const rootVirtualCode = (
  text: string,
  embeddedCodes: ReadonlyArray<LoomVirtualCode>,
  symbols: ReadonlyArray<Mapping> = [],
): LoomVirtualCode => ({
  id: 'root',
  languageId: 'loom',
  code: text,
  mappings: [
    {
      genStart: 0,
      genLength: text.length,
      source: {
        start: { line: 1, offset: 0 },
        end: { line: 1, offset: text.length },
      },
      kind: 'source',
    },
    ...symbols,
  ],
  embeddedCodes,
})

export const rootNamesAt = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  path: Location,
): ReadonlySet<string> => {
  const here = modules.get(path)?.product?.code ?? []
  const named = new Set(
    Array.getSomes(
      here
        .flatMap((code) => code.fragments)
        .map((part) =>
          part.type === 'NameRef'
            ? part.target.pipe(
                Option.filter((t) => t.path === path),
                Option.map((t) => t.name.toLowerCase()),
              )
            : Option.none(),
        ),
    ),
  )
  return new Set(here.map((code) => code.origin.name.toLowerCase()).filter((id) => !named.has(id)))
}

## The service

`LoomVirtualCodeBuilder` wraps `fromProduct` as an Effect service for callers already inside Effect — the corpus compiler; the single-file editor path uses the functions directly.

=>

export class LoomVirtualCodeBuilder extends Context.Service<LoomVirtualCodeBuilder>()(
  'LoomVirtualCodeBuilder',
  {
    make: Effect.succeed({
      fromProduct: (
        modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
        root: SectionId,
      ): Effect.Effect<LoomVirtualCode> => Effect.sync(() => fromProduct(modules, root)),
    }),
  },
) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

## What this module draws on

Effect supplies `Array`, `Option`, and `pipe` for the folds and the resolution, and `Effect` itself for the service. `byType`, from the node foundation, tells a fragment from a composing edge and a module anchor. The de re node types, the `Part` that unites them, `keyOf`, and `isMeant` come from `ProductAst`, with the `Provided` names an edge records, `Position` from the foundation, and the `LoomModule` and `Location` the resolution walks from the corpus model, with the `Catalogue` an open edge resolves through and `catalogueOf`, which builds it. The product pass's `targetOf` binds an open edge, for the walk and for a contract alike, and its `moduleFileOf`, with the `ModuleFile` it answers, tells whether a module anchor's module is to be settled and which file a module anchor's contract covers, over the `Landing` that `landingOf` builds. The parse `LoomDocument` comes from the AST, and `symbolsOf` from `LoomSymbol` classifies its tokens for the symbol mappings. The virtual-code types come from `LoomVirtualCode`.

=>

import { Array, Context, Effect, Layer, Option, pipe } from 'effect'
import {
  isMeant,
  keyOf,
  type Code,
  type Fragment,
  type ModuleRef,
  type Part,
  type Provided,
  type Ref,
  type SectionId,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/ProductAst'
import { byType, type Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import {
  catalogueOf,
  type Catalogue,
  type LoomModule,
  type Location,
  promisedThrough,
  type TropeContract,
  tropeContractsIn,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import {
  type Landing,
  landingOf,
  ModuleFile,
  moduleFileOf,
  sectionNameOf,
  targetOf,
} from '#ast/ProductBuilder'
import { type LoomDocument } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import { symbolsOf } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomSymbol'
import {
  type LoomVirtualCode,
  type Mapping,
  type MappingKind,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomVirtualCode'

